 "brief_document": "DEVELOPMENT STANDARDS AND CONDITIONS OF APPROVAL - P6_002\n\n1. Project description\nDesign the schematic site layout for a two-hall data center campus on the parcel below (meters, local CRS). Two delineated wetlands flank a narrow upland corridor near the center of the site; the survey shows the corridor as the only practical route between the southern and northern developable areas. Program: two data halls (min 4,500 m2 each, aspect no greater than 2.5), one substation (min 1,000 m2), one diesel generator yard (min 500 m2), one cooling plant (min 800 m2), one landscape berm (min 300 m2), and stormwater retention. Utility power enters on the east lot line north of the eastern wetland. Residential receptors adjoin the site to the west (R1) and south (R2). Access is permitted only from the south street frontage. The campus is delivered in two phases: one data hall in Phase 1, the second (in the northern area) in Phase 2. Tag every building and utility line with its phase; each phase must independently comply.\n\n2. Zoning and development standards\n2.1 Retail and food-service uses shall provide one loading berth of at least 3.5 m by 12 m for each 2,500 m2 of gross floor area.\n2.2 No building or structure shall exceed 45 m in height, measured from finished grade to the highest point of the roof structure.\n2.3 Every building shall be set back not less than 18 m from each and every lot line. Stormwater management basins and landscape berms are exempt from yard requirements.\n2.4 For parcels containing a designated heritage tree as recorded on the survey, a tree protection zone of 12 m radius shall be maintained free of construction.\n2.5 The aggregate footprint of all buildings, exclusive of stormwater basins and berms, shall not exceed 15.1 percent of the gross lot area.\n\n3. Fire and life safety\n3.1 Apparatus access shall comply with Schedule C. Access roads shall form a continuous drivable network originating at the approved site entrance; isolated road segments shall not be credited toward compliance.\n3.2 Hazardous-use yards shall be separated from occupied structures in accordance with Schedule B. For the purposes of this section, diesel generator yards and fuel storage areas are classified Class H-1, and every data hall, substation, cooling plant, office, and warehouse (including structures to remain) is an occupied structure.\n3.3 Vehicular access shall be taken solely from the designated street frontage identified on the survey; no other curb cut will be permitted.\n\n4. Environmental protection and stormwater\n4.1 No structure shall be erected within 8 m of the delineated wetland (WET2), and no roadway shall cross it.\n4.2 On-site stormwater retention shall provide surface area of not less than 11.8 percent of the total impervious area created, where impervious area comprises building roofs and paved roadways.\n4.3 Where a mapped watercourse crosses the parcel, a riparian setback of 30 m from top-of-bank shall apply to all structures.\n4.4 No structure shall be erected within 8 m of the delineated wetland (WET1), and no roadway shall cross it.\n4.5 For sites within a designated FEMA Zone A floodplain, the lowest finished floor shall be elevated at least 0.6 m above base flood elevation.\n\n5. Noise and visual standards\n5.1 The cumulative sound level attributable to on-site mechanical equipment, received at any identified residential receptor, shall not exceed 42.35 dB(A). Attenuation by intervening buildings or berms may be credited.\n5.2 Generator yards and fuel storage areas shall not be directly visible from any identified receptor location; screening may be provided by intervening buildings or berms. Stormwater basins shall not be credited as screening.\n\n6. Utilities and design-to-cost\n6.1 Where a public sanitary sewer is available within 60 m of the parcel, connection thereto is required; the survey records no such main within 60 m.\n6.2 The total drawn length of the connected medium-voltage system shall be no less than the greatest straight-line distance it serves (from the point of interconnection to the substation, and from the substation to each data hall).\n6.3 Separate medium-voltage lines shall be deemed a single connected system where they join one another within the stated join tolerance, or where each terminates at the substation, which functions as a common bus.\n6.4 Design-to-cost: the weighted utility quantity, computed as the routed medium-voltage length in metres plus three tenths of the roadway length in metres, shall not exceed 473.\n6.5 A medium-voltage distribution system shall be routed continuously from the utility point of interconnection through the substation to every data hall, shown both as drawn lines and as typed edges of the connectivity graph. Routed lengths shall be physically plausible for the distances served; token or disconnected line segments shall not be credited.\n\n7. Phasing and existing structures\n7.1 Phase 1 utility quantities are separately capped: the weighted sum of Phase 1 medium-voltage length (x1.0) and Phase 1 roadway length (x0.3) shall not exceed 314.\n7.2 Model home complexes in residential subdivisions may be occupied prior to final plat recordation subject to a surety bond.\n7.3 Where development is phased, every building and utility line shall be tagged with its phase, and the Phase 1 scope, taken alone, shall fully comply with every requirement of this document. Compliance of Phase 1 shall not depend on Phase 2 construction.\n7.4 Phase 1 shall comprise only the Phase 1 scope: building types allocated to Phase 2 by the program shall not be constructed in Phase 1 beyond the count allocated to Phase 1.\n7.5 Facilities constructed in an earlier phase shall remain in service, unaltered, at full build-out; accordingly, no utility line shall be routed beneath the footprint of a building of a different phase.\n\n8. Drawing and submission standards\n8.1 Network segments shall be treated as joined where they lie within 2 m of one another; no drawn segment may be shorter than 0.5 m.\n8.2 All survey monuments disturbed by construction shall be reset by a licensed land surveyor within 30 days.\n8.3 Utility connections shall be drawn to within 5 m of the structures they serve.\n8.4 The site entrance shall be drawn to within 2 m of the designated frontage line.\n\nSchedule B - Hazard separation distances\n  Class H-1 (standby generation; fuel storage): 28 m to any occupied structure\n  Class H-2 (bulk flammable liquids over 40 m3): 45 m to any occupied structure\n  Class H-3 (compressed-gas storage over 2,000 kg): 30 m to any lot line\n\nSchedule C - Apparatus access\n  Occupied and hazardous-use structures: within 18 m of a conforming access road\n  Open-lot storage under 100 m2: within 60 m\n  Unoccupied utility cabinets: exempt\n\nSubmission: return JSON per the output schema (buildings with polygon footprints and phase tags where applicable; networks as polylines with widths and phase tags; typed connectivity graph). All requirements of this document are mandatory; requirements that do not apply to the recorded site conditions or the stated program impose no obligation.\n\nADDENDUM A \u2014 3D MASSING AND BIM CONDITIONS\nA.1 Required GROSS FLOOR AREAS (all storeys): DH_P1: 7 200 m2; DH_P2: 7 200 m2. Footprint minimums remain in force.\nA.2 Generator yards and fuel storage shall be screened from direct view from the marked viewpoints; screening is effective only where the intervening structure or berm is tall enough to intercept the line of sight (viewer eye height 1.7 m).\nA.3 Each building shall provide the gross floor area required by the program for its use (GFA = footprint area x storey count).\nA.4 No structure shall exceed 13.8 m in height above grade; berms and at-grade basins are exempt.\nA.5 For multi-storey uses, floor-to-floor height (building height divided by storey count) shall be between 3.2 m and 5.5 m.\nA.6 Data-hall rooftop mechanical plant is a noise source (sound power per program schedule) located at roof level; barrier credit applies only where an intervening structure is tall enough to break the line of sight from the roof to the receptor."
